 :adr/title "What a dependency-coordinate rename actually costs — four layers, measured by walking into each one"
 :adr/status "accepted"
 :adr/date "2026-09-07"
 :adr/deciders ["Jun Kawasaki" "Claude Opus 5"]
 :adr/repos ["com-junkawasaki/root" "kotoba-lang/osaho" "kotoba-lang/amu"]
 :adr/related ["adr-2609078000-kir-is-renamed-osaho" "adr-2609076000-kir-is-an-ipld-codec-schema-and-adl"]
 :adr/body "## Context\n\nRenaming `kotoba-kir` to `osaho` looked like a one-line follow-up: GitHub redirects, so the ~79 repositories naming `io.github.kotoba-lang/kotoba-kir` keep resolving, and migrating the coordinate is tidying. It was estimated as one wave of `deps.edn` edits.\n\nThat estimate was wrong by three layers. Each was found by walking into it, not by thinking harder first, and each is recorded here because the next rename will meet them in the same order.\n\n## The four layers\n\n**1. Declarations.** `deps.edn`, `nbb.edn`, `shadow-cljs.edn`. This is the layer everyone estimates. Measured: 70 files across 66 repositories, landed in one pass through the Contents API.\n\n**2. Assertions.** Code that names the coordinate as a VALUE — `(contains? prod-deps 'io.github.kotoba-lang/kotoba-kir)`, `(dependency-pin 'io.github.kotoba-lang/kotoba-kir)`, a workflow's `--override-deps`. **These do not appear in a `deps.edn` search, because they are not declarations.** Nine files across seven repositories. They broke `main` in two repositories and stayed broken until CI said so.\n\n> A rename is not finished when every declaration moves. A test that pins the old NAME is not a declaration.\n\n**3. Pins.** A consumer that renames its own coordinate while pinning dependencies at shas that still name the OLD one gets **both** in its resolved closure. Measured on amu: the regenerated lock carried `kotoba-kir`@b021a0d1 transitively beside `osaho`@658436f2 directly, and the older one loads — see the mechanism below. So the rename cannot land in a consumer without advancing that consumer's pins into the migrated dependencies.\n\n**4. Whatever the pin advance was holding back.** amu's eight relevant pins were 20, 11, 7, 4, 3, 3, 1 and 1 commits behind. The rename did not create that debt; it made the wave walk into it. Two of amu's own gates fired (a pin asserted by sha, and the native loader's source identity), and a genuine behaviour regression appeared in the browser suite that no amount of coordinate work can fix.\n\n## The mechanism, measured\n\ntools.deps treats the coordinate SYMBOL as the library's identity. `:git/url` is not. So two names for one repository are two libraries:\n\n- both are checked out and **both land on one classpath**, no error and no warning;\n- with divergent shas, the one whose lib name sorts FIRST is the one that loads, and swapping declaration order does not change it;\n- newest-sha-wins resolves per lib name, so it never fires across a rename.\n\n`kotoba-kir` sorts before `osaho`. **The old name always shadows the new one.** In amu this surfaced as `native target profile does not match target identity` — the old KIR shadowing the new one that the pinned native backend expected.\n\n## Decisions\n\n**1. A coordinate rename is all-or-nothing across a closure, and the unit is the closure, not the repository.** A partial migration is strictly worse than none, because the pre-migration fleet is uniform on one name and therefore safe. Do not start one without a way to prove it finished.\n\n**2. `verify-dep-coordinate-identity` is that proof, and it groups by the CURRENT GitHub name.** It previously grouped by the `:git/url` string, which finds an old name only while the entry still carries the old url — the moment a consumer updates both, the pair goes invisible. Fixed 2026-09-07 after a synthetic half-migrated tree was reported clean.\n\n**3. Search four layers before estimating, not one.** Declarations, assertions, pins, and the pin advance's own cost. The first is the one that gets counted; the fourth is the one that decides how long it takes.\n\n**4. Do not paste an actual over an expected to clear a boundary gate.** amu's `pinned-closure-carries-the-complete-native-boundary` and `pure-native-pilot-suite` pin a sha and a loader source identity. Every past advance in that file carries a written justification of what it enables, down to which byte of which encoding inverts under a one-bit change. A rename is not that justification. Those gates were left red rather than satisfied.\n\n**5. Coupled pins cannot be bisected.** amu's own contract requires `kotoba-sema`, `kotoba-kir`, `kotoba-native` and `kotoba-verifier` to move in one commit, because a frontend that admits what native cannot encode is the gap the suite exists to measure. Two bisect halves were run before that was read; both split the coupling, so both reds describe states the contract forbids and neither could name a cause. **Read the gate before designing the experiment.**\n\n## What this ADR does not claim\n\n- The browser regression is not diagnosed here. It is in the pin adoption, outside the coupled three on the evidence so far, and it is upstream work that the rename surfaced rather than caused.\n- amu is not migrated. The fleet is therefore mid-wave, which is the state decision 1 says to avoid — recorded rather than hidden.\n\n## Measurement errors made while doing this\n\nBoth are the class this workspace keeps naming, made by the agent applying the rule:\n\n- **A CI status query counted still-running checks as failures.** GitHub returns `\"\"` for a running check's conclusion, and the predicate was `!= \"SUCCESS\"`. Thirteen checks were reported red when one was. Compare on `status == \"COMPLETED\"` first, then read `conclusion`.\n- **`grep -c` prints `0` and exits 1**, so `$(grep -c … || echo 0)` yields `\"0\\n0\"` and every name tested as taken. The availability check said 21 of 21 names were taken; the real answer was 3.\n\nBoth printed a confident wrong answer rather than failing. Neither had a floor."}]
